<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- The Mac Catalyst half of App/BrassPawn.entitlements. Two differences,
	     both forced by the platform rather than chosen:

	     The sandbox is not optional. A Mac App Store build is refused without
	     it; the keys below grant the capabilities used by the app.

	     The App Group is gone. It existed so the App Clip could leave an
	     invitation where the app would find it, and a Mac has no App Clip to
	     leave one. SharedContainer falls back to the app's own container when
	     no group is available, so the app still hands an invitation from the
	     link that opened it to the screen that acts on it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Game Center and StoreKit both talk to Apple. Neither works in a
	     sandbox that cannot open an outbound connection, and Online play is
	     the whole of one mode. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- And the sandbox has to let something in as well, which is what this
	     file used to say it did not.

	     Multiplayer is real-time Game Center: GKMatchmaker finds the
	     opponent, and the GKMatch then carries the game, the opponent's moves
	     arriving through GKMatchDelegate. Apple's "Initializing and
	     configuring Game Center" says it plainly: for Mac targets, enable
	     both Incoming Connections and Outgoing Connections under App
	     Sandbox > Network. With the client entitlement alone, a Mac was
	     paired and then never connected.

	     App Review's automated check flagged this key (2.4.5, 1 October
	     2026) as unused; the answer, and the review notes, say what it is
	     for. Keep it. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- Reading a position off a photograph of a real board, on a Mac that
	     has a camera pointed at one. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
	<true/>

	<!-- PhotoBoardSheet uses the system PhotosPicker and loads only the
	     selected item's Data through loadTransferable. It does not read the
	     Pictures folder or retain access to a file URL, so no Pictures,
	     user-selected file, or security-scoped bookmark entitlement is needed. -->

	<key>com.apple.developer.game-center</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
